      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1928 the community moved one mile south to stay connected with the U.S. rail line, a move that kept the town alive for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        23,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokio%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 2000 only 24 residents remained, leaving weathered buildings that now tell the story of a once-thriving farming community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/tokio-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A historical marker stands in front of the old Tokio School, commemorating the town’s educational heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokio%2C_Texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cotton farming and livestock were the economic backbone of Tokio before its gradual decline in the late twentieth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
